WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.101
Hey Crazies.

00:00:01.101 --> 00:00:09.709
The James Webb Space Telescope, also known as JWST or “Webb” for short, has been giving us incredible images for a little over a year now.

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:13.346
We've seen some of the first galaxies to ever form,

00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:17.584
and we've gotten to look at our local space in an entirely new light.

00:00:17.584 --> 00:00:18.718
Pun intended.

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:25.025
But between ballooning budgets, a decade and a half of launch delays and a controversy over the name,

00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:27.927
getting this thing into space was kind of a nightmare.

00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:31.431
[Intro Music]

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:37.837
You may have heard the JWST was meant as a replacement for Hubble, but that's only partially true.

00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:38.705
To the timeline!

00:00:39.139 --> 00:00:49.849
Hubble was launched in 1990 and the Space Telescope Science Institute, or STScI, was already discussing the next mission seven months earlier in September of 1989.

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:55.055
At the time, it was called the Next Generation Space Telescope or NGST.

00:00:55.055 --> 00:00:57.557
We'll get to the whole naming drama a little later.

00:00:57.557 --> 00:00:59.092
It was a whole thing.

00:00:59.092 --> 00:01:03.530
Anyway, this 1989 meeting was basically just a brainstorming session,

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:07.534
which means they lived by rule number four: Go big or go home.

00:01:07.534 --> 00:01:16.910
They imagined a space telescope that had a ten meter wide primary mirror with sensors for all visible, infrared, and ultraviolet wavelengths.

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:20.346
This was way too ambitious and those plans did not last long.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:25.985
By 1993, the ten meter mirror had already been downgraded to an eight meter one,

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:29.589
which is actually a meter and a half larger than it ended up being.

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:34.661
And, by 1997, NASA had also decided to scale back the sensor range.

00:01:34.661 --> 00:01:39.566
That's when it became clear this next generation telescope would not be a Hubble replacement.

00:01:39.566 --> 00:01:45.472
Hubble could see slightly into the infrared, but its main focus was visible and ultraviolet light.

00:01:45.472 --> 00:01:49.943
The plan for this new telescope was to focus entirely on infrared.

00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:53.079
Plus, it was set to launch well before we retired Hubble.

00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:58.585
By 1999, NASA had some thorough designs and an estimated timeline.

00:01:58.585 --> 00:02:03.723
Launch would happen in 2007 with a total cost of $1 billion (US).

00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:06.493
*Laughs*
Yeah, neither of those happened.

00:02:06.493 --> 00:02:08.561
The cost quickly ballooned.

00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:11.164
A year later, the budget had almost doubled.

00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:14.400
By 2005, the cost had more than tripled.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.439
By 2008, a year after the original launch estimate, it had quintupled.

00:02:19.439 --> 00:02:25.044
By 2011, it had risen to $8 billion (US) and it was nowhere near launching.

00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:29.315
This is when US Congress temporary canceled the project.

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:31.651
I thought NASA was part of the executive branch.

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:36.789
Sure, NASA might be run by the executive branch, but Congress controls the budget.

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:40.160
Politics are a recurring theme in this video. Settle in.

00:02:40.393 --> 00:02:44.664
Later in 2011, NASA managed to change Congress's mind on the project

00:02:44.664 --> 00:02:48.401
by agreeing to limit the total budget to $8 billion (US).

00:02:48.401 --> 00:02:49.869
No more ballooning.

00:02:49.869 --> 00:02:54.340
Just for some perspective, that's billion with a B, as in a thousand million.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.676
Yeah, the budget cap was eight of those.

00:02:56.676 --> 00:02:59.279
Spoiler Alert: They did not keep their promise.

00:02:59.312 --> 00:03:02.649
By the time it finally launched in December of 2021,

00:03:02.649 --> 00:03:05.618
14 years after the original estimate,

00:03:05.618 --> 00:03:09.589
JWST's total cost was about $10 billion (US).

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:11.724
It's not like that was spent all at once though.

00:03:11.724 --> 00:03:15.061
That's true. The cost was spread over a couple of decades.

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:17.630
Even at peak spending in the mid 20 teens,

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:22.468
JWST never accounted for more than 4% of NASA's annual budget,

00:03:22.468 --> 00:03:27.473
which itself was always less than 1% of overall government spending at the time.

00:03:27.473 --> 00:03:35.081
So, while $10 billion (US) seems like a lot to an average person like us, it's not really that much to a government, especially if it's spread out.

00:03:35.081 --> 00:03:39.118
But the fact that this project was spread out also made it cost more.

00:03:39.319 --> 00:03:44.324
As John Morse would say, the cheapest JWST is the one that launches the soonest.

00:03:44.657 --> 00:03:47.193
Nature wasn't exactly on our side, though.

00:03:47.193 --> 00:03:50.763
We almost lost the telescope to a hurricane in 2017.

00:03:50.763 --> 00:03:55.201
Then a few years later, the COVID-19 pandemic hit and production stalled.

00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:58.871
We also had to be absolutely sure this thing was going to work.

00:03:58.871 --> 00:04:03.743
When we launched Hubble back in 1990, the first pictures were blurrier than expected.

00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:08.815
Luckily, we designed Hubble to be serviceable and launched a repair mission in 1993.

00:04:08.815 --> 00:04:10.950
We basically gave it glasses.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:13.086
Couldn't we also do that with JWST?

00:04:13.086 --> 00:04:14.654
Unfortunately, no.

00:04:14.654 --> 00:04:17.523
This telescope had to be much farther away.

00:04:17.657 --> 00:04:22.662
It's supposed to be sensitive to infrared, but that's the kind of light warm things emit.

00:04:22.662 --> 00:04:27.834
If we put the telescope in orbit around Earth, it would be passing in and out of Earth's shadow.

00:04:27.834 --> 00:04:32.272
The temperature of the telescope itself would change and the sensors would pick that up.

00:04:32.272 --> 00:04:34.274
It would essentially make it blind.

00:04:34.274 --> 00:04:38.544
We needed this telescope to be thermally stable and as cold as possible.

00:04:38.678 --> 00:04:43.349
So, early on the plan was to launch JWST out to L2,

00:04:43.349 --> 00:04:45.485
one of Earth's Lagrange points.

00:04:45.485 --> 00:04:50.356
This would keep it out of Earth's shadow and oriented away from the Sun at all times.

00:04:50.356 --> 00:04:54.594
All the way out here, JWST would be impossible to service.

00:04:54.594 --> 00:04:59.899
It had to work perfectly at launch, or we just wasted billions of dollars and decades of work.

00:04:59.932 --> 00:05:03.002
So, most of the delays were justified paranoia.

00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:05.805
But then the name of the telescope became an issue.

00:05:06.372 --> 00:05:10.043
NASA had renamed the telescope all the way back in 2002.

00:05:10.043 --> 00:05:18.451
It was christened the James Webb Space Telescope, in honor of James Webb, who served as the NASA administrator from 1961 to 1968.

00:05:18.785 --> 00:05:20.219
Those were the days of the space race,

00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:22.955
the era of Projects Mercury and Gemini.

00:05:22.955 --> 00:05:25.725
We're talking the first humans in space.

00:05:25.725 --> 00:05:27.994
The sixties were a big deal for NASA.

00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:32.832
Seemed like an easy sell, but then an inflammatory quote surfaced in 2015.

00:05:32.832 --> 00:05:37.437
I'm not going to read it aloud because I can't stomach it. It's terribly homophobic.

00:05:37.437 --> 00:05:40.039
But feel free to pause and read if you want.

00:05:40.106 --> 00:05:42.008
That's probably not even a real quote.

00:05:42.008 --> 00:05:47.980
Fair. Just because you read a quote on the internet, that doesn't make it true. But NASA had to investigate it nonetheless.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:52.285
You can't go naming your new revolutionary telescope after a homophobe, now can you?

00:05:52.285 --> 00:05:54.854
So NASA was faced with two questions.

00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:58.491
Did anyone actually say it? And was it James Webb?

00:05:58.491 --> 00:06:03.830
NASA thoroughly investigated both questions and released a 90 page report with sources.

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:05.965
Link in the doobly-do if you're interested.

00:06:05.965 --> 00:06:11.137
The conclusion of that investigation is that the quote is real, but James Webb didn't say it.

00:06:11.637 --> 00:06:12.872
Here's what happened.

00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:17.410
This whole thing blew up when Forbes ran a story on it in the summer of 2015.

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:23.583
But the earliest story seems to be from earlier that year in the Stranger, a local paper in Seattle.

00:06:23.683 --> 00:06:27.553
Who, in turn, based their story on a quote from Wikipedia.

00:06:27.553 --> 00:06:29.655
Yes, that Wikipedia.

00:06:29.655 --> 00:06:34.761
Now, just because a quote is on Wikipedia, that doesn't make it wrong, but you should always fact check it.

00:06:34.761 --> 00:06:36.729
Come with me down the rabbit hole.

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:42.435
Wikipedia attributed this quote to James Webb and added it to his biography page in 2011.

00:06:42.435 --> 00:06:47.607
The source listed for it was a book titled Toward Stonewall, published in 2003.

00:06:47.607 --> 00:06:53.045
But the author only says that an under secretary of state testified at a Senate hearing.

00:06:53.045 --> 00:06:58.384
And that the quote is from the Senate committee's report, not the under secretary himself.

00:06:58.384 --> 00:07:05.691
Though it does say this unnamed under secretary had already fired many State Department employees simply for being homosexual.

00:07:06.025 --> 00:07:13.166
That hearing took place in February of 1950 and, at that time, James Webb was the under secretary of state.

00:07:13.166 --> 00:07:15.535
Well, there you have it. Time to get angry!

00:07:15.535 --> 00:07:17.537
No, no. Sweet summer child.

00:07:17.537 --> 00:07:22.141
Allow my friend Mr. Beat to enlighten you on the rankings inside the State Department.

00:07:22.141 --> 00:07:23.209
Thanks, Nick.

00:07:23.209 --> 00:07:27.313
So the president doesn't have to govern the United States alone.

00:07:27.313 --> 00:07:30.716
He gets lots of help and not just from the vice president.

00:07:30.716 --> 00:07:35.354
In fact, there is a huge team working with them.

00:07:35.354 --> 00:07:39.025
It's commonly referred to as the cabinet.

00:07:39.025 --> 00:07:44.430
Today, the cabinet officially includes the heads of 15 executive departments.

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:48.201
No, not just their heads, their whole body.

00:07:48.201 --> 00:07:50.236
This isn't a Futurama episode, you know what I mean?

00:07:50.236 --> 00:07:55.174
Like heads, like they're in charge of 15 executive departments.

00:07:56.309 --> 00:08:03.850
Anyway, they preside over millions of employees and hundreds of billions of dollars.

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:11.591
One of those executive departments, probably, arguably the most important one, is the State Department.

00:08:11.591 --> 00:08:19.632
The person in charge of the State Department is the secretary of state, who is currently this dude: Antony Blinken.

00:08:19.632 --> 00:08:25.171
Now, under the secretary of state is the deputy secretary of state.

00:08:25.171 --> 00:08:28.341
And, just to make this more confusing for you,

00:08:28.341 --> 00:08:30.943
underneath the deputy secretary of state

00:08:30.943 --> 00:08:34.413
are the under secretaries of state.

00:08:34.413 --> 00:08:37.650
And under the under secretaries of state

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:41.387
are the assistant secretaries of state.

00:08:41.387 --> 00:08:45.491
I could go into more detail about all of this for the next

00:08:45.591 --> 00:08:48.227
15 hours or so.

00:08:48.227 --> 00:08:49.495
How does that sound, Nick?

00:08:49.495 --> 00:08:51.597
No, no, that's plenty deep enough. Thanks, Matt.

00:08:51.597 --> 00:08:53.733
Are you sure?

00:08:53.733 --> 00:08:55.334
I've got nothing better to do.

00:08:55.334 --> 00:08:58.604
Dude. Go outside and touch some grass or something. Okay?

00:09:00.473 --> 00:09:02.241
But I don't have any grass.

00:09:02.241 --> 00:09:04.944
Anyway, James Webb held this job.

00:09:04.944 --> 00:09:07.013
It's now called the deputy secretary,

00:09:07.013 --> 00:09:09.916
but in his day it was called the under secretary.

00:09:09.916 --> 00:09:14.587
The titles changed in 1972, I'm guessing to be less confusing.

00:09:14.587 --> 00:09:17.156
You can kind of think of him as the second in command.

00:09:17.156 --> 00:09:20.960
He was to the secretary of state, what the vice president is to the president.

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:23.629
He was like a backup secretary of state.

00:09:23.629 --> 00:09:26.933
So, you remember that Senate hearing back in February of 1950?

00:09:26.933 --> 00:09:28.768
Yeah, James Webb wasn't even there.

00:09:28.768 --> 00:09:31.537
The secretary of state, Dean Acheson, was there

00:09:31.537 --> 00:09:36.442
and so was John Peurifoy, the under secretary of state for management.

00:09:36.442 --> 00:09:41.547
That position manages the internal workings of the State Department, especially personnel.

00:09:41.547 --> 00:09:45.151
He is the one that fired all those people in the years up to the hearing.

00:09:45.151 --> 00:09:49.755
And it was his testimony that made this hearing the start of the Lavender Scare.

00:09:49.755 --> 00:09:51.290
What's the Lavender Scare?

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:54.327
Okay. Fair. I guess this calls for some context.

00:09:54.327 --> 00:09:58.431
The mid-1900s in the U.S. were kind of a dumpster fire.

00:09:58.431 --> 00:10:00.866
There was a lot of social and political turmoil.

00:10:00.866 --> 00:10:04.870
It was a time of extreme paranoia, fear, and prejudice.

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:06.772
Isn't that the U.S. all the time?

00:10:06.772 --> 00:10:10.276
Well, it was particularly bad in the wake of World War II.

00:10:10.276 --> 00:10:15.581
We had something called the second Red Scare, where everyone was afraid of the rise of communism.

00:10:15.982 --> 00:10:17.984
Actually, I think I'm underselling this.

00:10:17.984 --> 00:10:21.587
Not just afraid. That's been pretty steady since World War I.

00:10:21.587 --> 00:10:23.923
We're talking full blown paranoia.

00:10:23.923 --> 00:10:28.361
People were accusing their friends, their neighbors, random people on the street.

00:10:28.361 --> 00:10:30.663
It was madness and it was bipartisan.

00:10:30.997 --> 00:10:35.167
In March of 1947, three years before that Senate hearing,

00:10:35.167 --> 00:10:39.271
President Truman had signed Executive Order 9835,

00:10:39.271 --> 00:10:45.911
calling for all government agencies to investigate their own employees for possible communist and subversive behavior.

00:10:45.911 --> 00:10:49.548
As you can imagine, this order was immediately abused.

00:10:49.548 --> 00:10:51.817
Subversive behavior. Unbelievable.

00:10:52.118 --> 00:10:54.687
Starting in the late forties and through the sixties,

00:10:54.687 --> 00:10:59.358
the line blurred between what was communist and what was morally objectionable.

00:10:59.358 --> 00:11:04.230
There was a mass purge of LGBTQ+ people from federal positions.

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:06.599
It's now known as the Lavender Scare.

00:11:06.599 --> 00:11:12.338
Were any of them communists? No, they weren't even all LGBTQ+, just suspected to be.

00:11:12.338 --> 00:11:15.708
But it was argued that their presence was subversive.

00:11:16.175 --> 00:11:19.011
The more I learn about American history, I swear.

00:11:19.011 --> 00:11:25.418
Anyway, this Peurifoy guy embraced Truman's executive order immediately and fired over 90 people

00:11:25.418 --> 00:11:29.822
between that order in 1947 and the Senate hearing in 1950.

00:11:29.822 --> 00:11:33.059
Couldn't James Webb or even President Truman have put a stop to it?

00:11:33.059 --> 00:11:38.998
According to the records, neither James Webb nor President Truman even knew about the firings until the Senate hearing.

00:11:38.998 --> 00:11:40.499
By then it was too late.

00:11:40.499 --> 00:11:43.736
Once Congress knew about it, all Truman could do was react.

00:11:43.736 --> 00:11:45.771
He had a meeting with Webb about the issue,

00:11:45.771 --> 00:11:51.243
and they did everything they could just to keep the personnel investigations internal to the State Department.

00:11:51.243 --> 00:11:55.715
The alternative was the Senate committee getting the records and investigating everyone themselves,

00:11:55.715 --> 00:11:58.684
which the president saw as unacceptable overreach.

00:11:58.684 --> 00:12:02.955
Congress agreed to the president's terms, as long as they got regular updates,

00:12:02.955 --> 00:12:08.360
and as long as John Peurifoy and his successors had full authority to fire whoever they wanted.

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:12.231
After that, Webb's involvement in the matter ends. It was out of his hands.

00:12:12.231 --> 00:12:15.768
Ultimately, it didn't even matter that they tried to cooperate with Congress.

00:12:16.102 --> 00:12:19.939
Two years later, James Webb resigned and went to work in the private sector.

00:12:19.939 --> 00:12:25.177
When Truman's second term was up in 1953, the executive branch switched parties.

00:12:25.177 --> 00:12:29.482
The new President Eisenhower signed Executive Order 10450,

00:12:29.482 --> 00:12:35.988
which lists homosexuality explicitly as a national security risk, though they called it something else at the time.

00:12:35.988 --> 00:12:41.761
In reality, the only thing that made LGBTQ+ people a security risk was the Lavender Scare itself.

00:12:42.061 --> 00:12:45.698
By the time Webb came back to head NASA in 1961,

00:12:45.698 --> 00:12:48.434
that Eisenhower order was still in full effect.

00:12:48.434 --> 00:12:53.506
He worked hard at NASA to meet JFK’s goals for equal employment and civil rights.

00:12:53.506 --> 00:12:59.211
Unfortunately, due to executive order 10450, that could not include sexual orientation.

00:12:59.211 --> 00:13:06.685
This order would be at full strength until 1973, well after Webb came and went at NASA.

00:13:06.685 --> 00:13:11.857
This was a terrible time in American history, and these sorts of problems are bigger than one person.

00:13:11.857 --> 00:13:20.499
That's why, after reviewing 50,000 pages of documents, NASA's official report cleared Webb of any direct involvement in the Lavender Scare.

00:13:20.499 --> 00:13:23.669
In light of that, they chose to keep his name on the telescope.

00:13:23.669 --> 00:13:28.941
Anyway, after two decades of budget increases, delays, and a huge naming controversy,

00:13:28.941 --> 00:13:32.077
we finally have the most epic telescope ever built.

00:13:32.077 --> 00:13:35.414
Four different instruments for detecting infrared light,

00:13:35.414 --> 00:13:39.418
18 gold hexagonal mirrors (because hexagons are the bestagons,)

00:13:39.418 --> 00:13:43.422
all arranged in a 6.5 meter wide infrared reflector.

00:13:43.422 --> 00:13:45.691
I'd say it was a miracle it ever got off the ground,

00:13:45.691 --> 00:13:49.061
but a lot of very skilled and motivated people made it happen.

00:13:49.061 --> 00:13:51.664
The people at NASA are absolutely incredible.

00:13:51.664 --> 00:13:55.267
We'll have to wait and see what else JWST has in store for us.

